#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# maximal_by

#% text
# `maximal_by(xs, f)` gives every element of `xs` with the largest `f(x)`, in their order; `maximal_by(xs, f, n)` the `n` elements with the largest keys, largest first.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# For an association, the entries with the largest values.

#% item
# `n` may be `up_to(n)` (at most `n`); ties keep their order.

#% item
# `maximal_by(f)(xs)` is `maximal_by(xs, f)`.

#% item
# `max_by` gives the first such element alone.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
maximal_by([[a, 3], [b, 2], [a, 2], [d, 1], [b, 3]], last)
#% output
#= [[a, 3], [b, 3]]

#% cell
maximal_by([1, 5, 3, 5, 2], abs, 3)
#% output
#= [5, 5, 3]

#% cell
maximal_by(last)(assoc("a" -> [4, 1], "b" -> [2, 3]))
#% output
#= assoc("b" -> [2, 3])

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Symbolic keys are ordered canonically:

#% cell
maximal_by([[a, 1], [b, 1], [d, 2]], first)
#% output
#= [[d, 2]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `minimal_by`, `max_by`, `sort_by`
